questions.detailed:
A modal dialog blocks interaction with the rest of the page until dismissed. Requirements:

1. An **accessible name** (`aria-labelledby` pointing to its heading).
2. **Focus moves into** the dialog on open.
3. Focus is **contained** while open, and the background is not reachable or readable.
4. **Escape** closes it (unless the action is destructive and needs confirmation).
5. A visible **close** control.
6. Focus **returns** to the triggering element on close.

## Native `<dialog>` first

Opened with `showModal()`, the native element renders in the top layer, makes the rest of the document inert, handles Escape (the `cancel` event), and places focus inside. It has `role="dialog"` implicitly. Browsers have improved a lot here and support is broad in modern versions, but verify behavior in the browsers and screen readers you support.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";

export function Dialog({ open, onClose, title, children }: {
  open: boolean; onClose: () => void; title: string; children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDialogElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const el = ref.current;
    if (!el) return;
    if (open && !el.open) el.showModal();
    if (!open && el.open) el.close();
  }, [open]);

  return (
    <dialog ref={ref} aria-labelledby="dlg-title" onClose={onClose}>
      <h2 id="dlg-title">{title}</h2>
      {children}
      <button type="button" onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
    </dialog>
  );
}
```

The `close` event fires on Escape and `close()`, which keeps React state in sync. Browsers commonly restore focus to the previously focused element, but if the trigger can unmount, restore it yourself.

## When custom is needed

If you cannot use `<dialog>`, such as with an older support matrix, build it with `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, a focus trap, `inert` or `aria-hidden` on siblings, a portal and a scroll lock. A tested library, such as one from a vetted design system, is safer than rolling your own.

## Common mistakes

- Opening with `show()` (non-modal) and expecting modal behavior.
- No label or no initial focus target.
- Auto-focusing the close button for long content (focusing the heading or first field may suit better).
- Focus not returned, or lost when the trigger is removed.
- Nested scroll or page scroll behind the dialog.
- Using a modal where an inline disclosure would do.

React does not make a site inaccessible by itself, but single-page apps remove behaviors the browser provides on full page loads, so you must replace them.

## Practices

- **Native elements** in components. A `Button` component should render `<button>` and forward props and refs.
- **Route changes**: update `document.title`, then move focus to the new page's `h1` (with `tabIndex={-1}`) or a main wrapper, or announce through a live region.

```tsx
function PageHeading({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const ref = React.useRef<HTMLHeadingElement>(null);
  React.useEffect(() => { ref.current?.focus(); }, []);
  return <h1 ref={ref} tabIndex={-1}>{children}</h1>;
}
```

- **Dynamic content**: insert status messages into a pre-existing `role="status"` container. A live region added at the same time as its content is often not announced.
- **Focus**: restore focus when UI is removed, such as a closed panel or deleted list item.
- **IDs**: use `useId` rather than hard-coded ids.
- **Fragments and wrappers**: avoid extra `div`s inside `ul`, `table` or `dl`, since invalid structure damages semantics.
- **Events**: `onClick` on a `div` is a smell. Use `button` or `a`.
- **Images and icons**: pass `alt`, mark decorative SVGs `aria-hidden`.
- **Language**: set `lang` on `html` and on passages in other languages.

## Tooling

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y` | Static checks of JSX |
| Testing Library `getByRole` | Encourages role and name based queries, which doubles as an accessibility check |
| `jest-axe` or Playwright with axe | Automated rule checks in tests |
| Storybook a11y addon | Component-level checks |

```tsx
import { render } from "@testing-library/react";
import { axe } from "jest-axe";

it("has no detectable violations", async () => {
  const { container } = render(<ExploreButton onExplore={() => {}} />);
  expect(await axe(container)).toHaveNoViolations();
});
```

This check is a baseline only. Keyboard flows and announcements still need manual testing.

## Common mistakes

Not moving focus on navigation, third-party UI kits with unverified accessibility, and `aria-live` regions rerendered on every state change.

No single test type covers accessibility, so use layers.

| Method | Finds | Typical tools |
|---|---|---|
| Automated scanning | Missing labels and alt, invalid ARIA, duplicate ids, some contrast, structural issues | axe-core / axe DevTools, Lighthouse, WAVE, Pa11y |
| Keyboard testing | Focus order, traps, hidden or invisible focus, unreachable controls | A keyboard, no mouse |
| Screen-reader testing | Missing or wrong names, confusing announcements, incorrect states, navigation problems | NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, TalkBack |
| Visual review | Contrast, focus indicators, 200% text resize, reflow at 320px, text spacing, zoom | Browser dev tools, contrast checkers |
| User testing | Usability barriers that rules cannot detect | Sessions with disabled participants |

## Where each fits in the pipeline

1. **Developer**: linting (`jsx-a11y`), Storybook a11y checks, accessibility tree inspection.
2. **CI**: component and page-level axe tests, failing the build on new violations.
3. **Pre-release**: scripted manual keyboard and screen-reader tests of key journeys.
4. **Periodic**: expert audits and user research.

## Example automated test

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import AxeBuilder from "@axe-core/playwright";

test("dealer search page has no detectable WCAG A/AA violations", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/dealers");
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa", "wcag22aa"])
    .analyze();
  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
});
```

## Manual keyboard script

Tab through the page and check that the order is logical and the focus indicator is always visible. Operate every control with Enter, Space and arrow keys as appropriate, open and close every dialog and menu with the keyboard, and confirm focus returns correctly. Then check zoom at 200% and a 320px viewport.

## Common mistakes

Relying only on a Lighthouse score, testing only one screen reader, and testing only the happy path while skipping error states and dynamic content.
